## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect cross-repo blue/green deployments with per-env isolation and safe promotion gates, using remote state data sources across repos.

## Key Concepts
- Blue/Green deployment across environments
- Terraform data sources and remote state cross-repo access
- Per-env backends and promotion gating in CI
- Route53 weighted routing for traffic shifting

## Code Example
```javascript
data "terraform_remote_state" "network" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = { bucket = "infra-network-terraform" key = "prod/terraform.tfstate" region = "us-east-1" }
}
resource "aws_lb_target_group" "blue" { }
resource "aws_lb_target_group" "green" { }
resource "aws_route53_record" "prod" {
  name = "app-prod.example.com"
  type = "A"
  set_identifier = "prod-blue"
  weight = 100
  alias { name = aws_lb_prod.dns_name, zone_id = aws_lb_prod.zone_id, evaluate_target_health = true }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle drift if network outputs change?
- How do you automate health checks before promoting traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design multi-cloud Terraform architectures with per-tenant isolation, cross-cloud networking, and automated post-deployment validation including drift detection and reporting.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant Terraform Cloud workspaces with unique workspace keys
- Provider-agnostic networking module (AWS VPC, GCP VNet)
- Policy-as-code enforcement (OPA/Sentinel)
- Drift detection running after apply
- CI gates for tenant-scoped plans

## Code Example

```python
def detect_drift(desired_state, live_state):
    drift = {}
    for resource_id, desired_config in desired_state.items():
        live_config = live_state.get(resource_id)
        if not live_config:
            drift[resource_id] = "missing"
        elif live_config != desired_config:
            drift[resource_id] = {
                "expected": desired_config,
                "actual": live_config
            }
    return drift
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests experience with governance, cross-repo data sharing, and policy-as-code in real fintech environments.

## Key Concepts
- cost-governance patterns
- terraform_remote_state data source
- policy as code (OPA)
- CI gating of plans

## Code Example
```javascript
# Minimal budget fetch (pseudo-terraform)
data "terraform_remote_state" "budget" {
  backend = "s3"
  config = {
    bucket = "gov-budgets";
    key    = "costs/${var.env}.tfstate";
    region = "us-east-1";
  }
}
locals {
  monthly_budget = data.terraform_remote_state.budget.outputs["monthly_budget"]
}
```
```javascript
# Minimal OPA policy sketch
package terraform.costs
deny[msg] {
  planned_cost := input.planned_cost
  budget := input.monthly_budget
  planned_cost > budget
  msg = sprintf("Budget exceeded: %v > %v", planned_cost, budget)
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale this to handle multi-region budgets?
- How would you add tests to verify gating behavior in CI?
